## The idea

A normal `Queue<T>` plus **one lock** and the **Monitor wait/pulse** pair. A producer that finds the queue full **waits** (which releases the lock); a consumer that removes an item **pulses** to wake it. The same in reverse for an empty queue. Always re-check the condition in a **while loop** after waking, because another thread may have taken the slot first. `Complete()` sets a flag and wakes everyone: producers then fail, consumers drain what is left and then get false. Cancellation registers a callback that **wakes the waiters**, so they see the token and throw.

## Step by step

1. Fields: Queue<T>, capacity, a lock object, a completed flag.
2. Enqueue: lock; while full and not completed → check the token, then Monitor.Wait. If completed → throw InvalidOperationException. Otherwise enqueue and PulseAll.
3. TryDequeue: lock; while empty and not completed → check the token, then Monitor.Wait. If still empty → completed and drained → return false. Otherwise dequeue, PulseAll, return true.
4. Complete: lock, set completed, PulseAll — every sleeper wakes and re-checks.
5. Cancellation: ct.Register(Wake) where Wake takes the lock and calls PulseAll. A waiting thread wakes, ThrowIfCancellationRequested fires, and it leaves.
6. Use PulseAll, not Pulse: producers and consumers wait on the same lock, so a single Pulse could wake the wrong kind of thread and everyone sleeps forever.

## Worked example

**Input:** capacity 1; producer P and consumer C

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| P: Enqueue(1) | queue [1]; PulseAll |
| P: Enqueue(2) | full → Wait (lock released) |
| C: TryDequeue | takes 1; queue []; PulseAll wakes P |
| P wakes | re-checks: not full → enqueue 2 |
| Complete() | completed = true; PulseAll |
| C: TryDequeue ×2 | gets 2, then the queue is empty and completed → false |

**Result:** C receives 1 and 2, then exits cleanly

## Cost

Enqueue and TryDequeue are O(1) apart from waiting. PulseAll wakes every waiter, which costs some extra wake-ups under heavy contention — correct first, then optimise (two condition objects, or SemaphoreSlim). Memory O(capacity).

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
public sealed class BoundedBlockingQueue<T> {
    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private readonly Queue<T> _items = new Queue<T>();
    private readonly int _capacity;
    private bool _completed = false;

    public BoundedBlockingQueue(int capacity) {
        if (capacity <= 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(capacity));
        _capacity = capacity;
    }

    public int Count {
        get { lock (_lock) { return _items.Count; } }
    }

    public void Enqueue(T item, CancellationToken ct = default) {
        // If the token is cancelled while we sleep, wake us so we can notice.
        using (ct.Register(Wake)) {
            lock (_lock) {
                while (_items.Count == _capacity && !_completed) {
                    ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
                    Monitor.Wait(_lock);              // releases the lock while sleeping
                }
                if (_completed) throw new InvalidOperationException("queue is completed");

                _items.Enqueue(item);
                Monitor.PulseAll(_lock);              // a consumer may be waiting for an item
            }
        }
    }

    public bool TryDequeue(out T item, CancellationToken ct = default) {
        using (ct.Register(Wake)) {
            lock (_lock) {
                while (_items.Count == 0 && !_completed) {
                    ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
                    Monitor.Wait(_lock);
                }
                if (_items.Count == 0) {              // completed AND drained: nothing will ever come
                    item = default;
                    return false;
                }

                item = _items.Dequeue();
                Monitor.PulseAll(_lock);              // a producer may be waiting for space
                return true;
            }
        }
    }

    // No more items. Consumers still drain what is already in the queue.
    public void Complete() {
        lock (_lock) {
            _completed = true;
            Monitor.PulseAll(_lock);
        }
    }

    private void Wake() {
        lock (_lock) {
            Monitor.PulseAll(_lock);
        }
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Capacity 0? Dequeue after complete? Enqueue after complete?**

Reject; false when drained; throw

**Q: Cost of PulseAll?**

Wakes all waiters — fine small, use two conditions at scale

**Q: Two producers, queue has one slot.**

Both wait in the while loop; one wins

**Q: Could this deadlock?**

Not with one monitor and while-loops

**Q: Consumers 10× slower.**

Bounded capacity is back-pressure; block, drop or reject

**Q: Shutdown with items in flight.**

Complete, drain with a deadline

**Q: A poison item.**

Per-item catch, dead-letter

